          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "Trouble sleeping for three months is worth raising with a doctor. The emergency check found no urgent warning signs in what you described. That does not mean nothing is wrong, so seek medical advice if symptoms become severe, get worse, or feel new and unexplained.\n\nThe reference material describes insomnia as trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, even when you have enough time to sleep, along with effects during the day such as tiredness or trouble concentrating (*Insomnia*, General clinical reference). Chronic insomnia is defined as this happening at least three nights a week for at least three months (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*, sleep medicine guidance). The three-month length is one part of that definition. The definition also needs the nightly pattern and daytime effects. Only a clinician can judge whether they apply.\n\nThe same guidance says a clinician will usually look at a sleep diary over one to two weeks. They also check for conditions that often cause sleep trouble, including sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, depression, anxiety, pain, and effects from medicines. They review caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, and shift work. Keeping a simple sleep log before an appointment could help.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover treatment options in detail. The guidance title names cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment, but I could not check its contents here, so please ask a clinician about options.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nI kept the content and document names the same. I also reworded one sentence so it no longer suggests that you have chronic insomnia.",
