          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is medication the usual answer?",
          "answer": "For chronic insomnia, the guidance does not treat medication as the usual first answer. The retrieved passages from *Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment* (sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance) say that cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is first-line, ahead of medication. The guidance says its effects last after treatment ends, while medication effects generally do not. It also says CBT avoids the harms linked to long-term use of sleep medicines. This CBT program is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions. It includes steps such as using the bed only for sleep and limiting time in bed.\n\nThe same document warns that alcohol can seem to help with falling asleep but tends to break up sleep later in the night.\n\nI could not find out from these passages when medication might be added, what kinds exist, or how they are chosen. Please ask a clinician about those questions. Do not change or start any medicine on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
