          "answer": "The retrieved guidance on insomnia describes cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) as the main non-drug option. It is a package of components delivered together over about four to eight sessions. The passages from *Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment* name two components: stimulus control, which means using the bed only for sleep and leaving it when you cannot sleep, and sleep restriction, which means limiting time in bed and extending it as sleep improves. The list continues past the part I could retrieve, so I cannot tell you the full set of components.\n\nMy first search returned passages about unrelated conditions, such as meningitis, medical coding, and antibiotics, so I did not use them. A second search returned general guidance on other conditions, such as anxiety and osteoarthritis. Those passages do not describe insomnia treatment, so I did not use them either.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not say what other options exist for insomnia, how they compare, or which might suit you. Please ask a clinician or a sleep specialist about these.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
